<h2 id="h-what-is-mathews-bridge-lock">What Is the Mathews Bridge-Lock Sight System?</h2>
<p>Bridge-Lock is Mathews&rsquo; integrated mounting system. Instead of bolting a bracket to the side of the riser with two screws, a Bridge-Lock riser has a dovetail-shaped cutout. A sight with a matching dovetail bar slides into that cutout and is held by a single setscrew recessed into the riser above the bar.</p>
<p>Mathews introduced it on the V3X and has carried it forward. A Mathews parts listing for the Bridge-Lock sight setscrew names the V3X, Phase4, LIFT series, ARC and TITLE series. If you shoot a newer Mathews such as the <?= post_link('mathews-lift-29-5', 'Mathews Lift 29.5') ?>, your riser almost certainly has it, but check the bow&rsquo;s spec page or ask your dealer to be sure.</p>
<p>The claimed benefits are practical ones:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No external bracket</strong>, so the sight sits closer to the riser in a more compact package.</li>
  <li><strong>Better balance.</strong> Moving the sight&rsquo;s weight toward the center of the bow reduces the side-heavy feel of a big bracket sight.</li>
  <li><strong>Less felt vibration</strong>, according to Mathews.</li>
  <li><strong>Simple to remove.</strong> Loosen one screw and the sight slides out, handy for travel cases.</li>
</ul>
<?= callout('note', 'Bridge-Lock is optional', 'A Bridge-Lock riser is designed to make dovetail sights easy, not to lock you into them. Many Mathews owners still run a conventional bracket-mounted sight. Before buying one, look at your riser for the standard threaded sight holes or confirm the fit with your dealer.') ?>

