<p>Third-axis adjustment is worth paying for if you hunt from steep treestands or mountainsides. Micro-adjust pins make the gang-and-gap sight-in faster. And a sight that ships with clear instructions and spare fibers will save you headaches.</p>

<h2 id="h-package-sight-or-upgrade">Package Sight or Upgrade?</h2>
<p>Some Mathews bows are sold through dealers as packages that include a sight, rest and quiver, while others come bare. If you are weighing those options, read our guide to <?= post_link('understanding-mathews-compound-bow-packages', 'understanding Mathews compound bow packages') ?>.</p>
<p>A package sight is usually fine for starting out. Upgrade when you notice a specific limit: pins that are too dim at last light, no 3rd axis adjustment for long shots, or a bracket that makes the bow feel side-heavy. At Better Outdoors we recommend upgrading for a reason, not just for a new look.</p>

