<h2 id="h-mounting-and-sighting-in">Mounting and Sighting In Your New Sight</h2>
<p>For a Bridge-Lock sight, clean the dovetail, slide the bar in to the position you want, and tighten the setscrew to the recommended torque. Don&rsquo;t over-tighten. For a bracket sight, use both mounting screws and a dab of blue thread locker.</p>
<p>Then level the sight on all three axes and sight in from 10 yards out. Our step-by-step guide on <?= post_link('how-to-sight-in-a-compound-bow', 'how to sight in a compound bow') ?> covers gang and gap adjustments, slider tapes and the broadhead check.</p>
<p>If you travel with your bow, a Bridge-Lock sight is quick to remove and reinstall. Mark the bar position with a paint pen so it goes back in the same spot, and confirm your 20-yard pin after every trip. A protective <?= amz('bow sight cover', 'bow sight cover') ?> also helps shield fibers in transit.</p>

<h2 id="h-final-thoughts">Final Thoughts: Choosing the Best Bow Sight for Mathews Bows</h2>
<p>Start with your hunting style. Treestand and blind hunters do well with a fixed-pin sight and .019 pins. Western and long-range hunters often prefer a single pin slider with finer pins and 3rd axis adjustment. Then choose your mount: Bridge-Lock for the cleanest, most balanced fit on a recent Mathews, or a conventional bracket if you want a sight that moves easily between bows. Get those two decisions right and almost any quality sight will serve you well.</p>

